# Question 2 — Rental Accommodation System (gRPC)

A distributed rental platform for the Ministry of Tourism: Hosts list and
manage properties, Guests browse, search, and book them. Built in Ballerina
using gRPC for all inter-process communication.

## Team split
 
| Person  | Responsibility |

| Jedidja | `.proto` contract + gRPC server implementation |
| Petrus  | gRPC client + end-to-end integration test |

## Project layout

```
Question 2/
├── server/
│   ├── Ballerina.toml
│   ├── rental.proto            the contract — source of truth for everything
│   ├── <generated>_pb.bal      types + descriptor, generated by `bal grpc`
│   ├── store.bal                in-memory maps: properties, users, bookings
│   ├── validation.bal           date parsing, overlap checks, night counting
│   └── rental_service.bal       the actual RentalService — all 8 RPCs
└── client/
    ├── Ballerina.toml
    ├── <generated>_pb.bal      same generated file, copied over — the client
    │                            needs the message types and client stub too
    └── main.bal                  drives the full guest/host flow end-to-end
```

Server and client are two **separate** Ballerina packages, each with their
own `Ballerina.toml`, run as two separate processes. That's a deliberate
choice, not an accident — see "Why two packages" below.

## The contract (`rental.proto`)

8 RPCs covering everything the spec asked for:

| RPC                         | Type             |Purpose |

| `add_property`              | simple           | Host lists a new property, gets back a `property_id` |
| `create_users`              | client-streaming | bulk-register Hosts/Guests, one confirmation at the end |
| `update_property`           | simple           | Host edits a listing |
| `remove_property`           | simple           | Host deletes a listing, gets back their remaining available listings |
| `list_available_properties` | server-streaming | Guest browses, optionally filtered by location/price |
| `search_property`           | simple           | Guest looks up one property by id |
| `book_property`             | simple           | Guest holds dates in a temporary cart |
| `confirm_booking`           | simple           | finalizes a held booking — re-checks for overlaps, prices it |

All three RPC shapes required by the assignment (simple, client-streaming,
server-streaming) are represented.

## How to run it

**Terminal 1 — start the server first, and wait for it:**
```powershell
cd server
bal build
bal run
```
Leave this running. It's a long-lived listener, not a one-shot script.

**Terminal 2 — once the server's confirmed up, run the client:**
```powershell
cd client
bal build
bal run
```

The client runs the whole guest/host journey top to bottom in one go:
registers a Host and Guest, lists a property, browses available listings,
searches for it by id, then books and confirms a stay — printing the
result of every step. A clean run through all 6 steps with no errors is
"it's working."

## Why two packages (not one)

The client and server logic were originally written to run from a single
process (`bal run` doing both at once for convenience). That created a
startup race: the listener and the client's `main()` both fire as part of
the same `bal run`, with no guarantee the listener finishes binding before
the client dials in — the client would connect and get its socket slammed
shut before the server-side gRPC layer was ready to accept it. It failed
intermittently, which is worse than failing consistently, since the bug can
hide during a quick test and only show up in a live demo.

Splitting them into genuinely separate processes removes the race entirely,
and is also just what a real distributed system looks like: independent
services that don't share a startup sequence, talking over the network. It
doubles as a better presentation — server logs incoming calls in one
terminal while the client's output streams in the other, side by side.

## Known non-issues

`bal build` prints a handful of `HINT`-level messages about methods not
being `isolated`, meaning Ballerina won't run concurrent requests to them in
parallel. These are informational only — not warnings, not errors — and
don't affect correctness for a single-client demo. Left as-is.